Implement user story AMSD-2041: [GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Story Description
[GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Acceptance Criteria
- 


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is the single source that COMPUTES and PROVIDES all CMS content to the entire React tree (165+ consumers). Currently it only accepts a static defaultContent prop and wraps it in useMemo with no mechanism for real-time updates. Live Preview requires the provider to subscribe to Contentstack SDK events and re-derive content when the CMS pushes changes — that subscription and re-derivation must happen here, not in any downstream presentation component.
  - **Minimal fix:** Enhance ContentstackProvider to integrate Contentstack Live Preview: (1) Accept a livePreviewConfig prop (host, stackApiKey, deliveryToken, environment) on IContentStackProviderProps; (2) On mount, call Contentstack Live Preview SDK initialization (e.g. livePreview.init({ ...config, onChange: callback })) to register a listener; (3) Store live-preview-updated content in React state (useState) initialized from defaultContent; (4) In the SDK onChange callback, merge the incoming live preview data into state, replacing the static useMemo with this state-driven value so all 236+ useContent consumers re-render with updated data. The useMemo on line 56 should be replaced with state that updates on both defaultContent changes and live preview callbacks.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When a tester opens a page in the GO, UP, or MX experience, the CMS-driven page sections (titles, body copy, and other managed content) are visibly rendered on screen — no blank areas, placeholder text, or missing blocks.
- While the page is open in preview, a tester edits a piece of content in the CMS and saves it; the updated text then appears on the open page without the tester manually refreshing the browser.
- The text displayed on the page matches the content values the tester just authored in the CMS (e.g., the edited headline shows the new wording, not the previous wording).
- After the preview session is interrupted, the content that was already on screen remains visible and stable — the page does not go blank, crash, or display an error message.
- Each targeted experience (GO, UP, MX) displays its own locale-appropriate CMS content on screen, with no untranslated fallback or content from another region shown in its place.

## Tests are NOT your job this turn
A dedicated test-writer agent runs immediately after your fix commits and owns the bug-reproducing test. Do NOT write, edit, or create any test file (*.test.*, *.spec.*, __tests__/). Write ONLY the fix. Adding a test here wastes your turn budget and has caused repeated failures.

